WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:14.480
When people talk about big data, they nearly&nbsp;
always talk about data science, and data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:21.040
scientists, as well. Now, just as the definition&nbsp;
of big data is still debated, the same is true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:26.720
for the definition of data science. To some&nbsp;
people, the term’s just a fancier way of saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:33.120
statistics and statisticians. On the other hand,&nbsp;
other people argue that data science is a distinct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:38.960
field. It has different training, techniques,&nbsp;
tools and goals than statistics typically has.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:43.840
Now, that’s what we’re going to&nbsp;
talk about in this presentation.&nbsp;

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:51.200
The first thing that we want to do, is&nbsp;
we want to look at what’s called the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:57.440
data science venn diagram. This is a chart&nbsp;
that was created by Drew Conway in 2010,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:02.240
and what he’s arguing here is that data science&nbsp;
involves a combination of three different skills.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:09.040
The first is statistics. That’s on the&nbsp;
top right. The second, on the bottom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:12.880
is domain knowledge that you actually&nbsp;
know, for instance, about management or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:18.400
advertising. And the one on the top left is&nbsp;
coding, or being able to program computers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:24.080
He’s arguing that, to get data science, a person&nbsp;
needs to be able to do all three of these.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:29.920
So, we’re going to talk about each of these facets&nbsp;
one at a time and in combination with each other.&nbsp;

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:36.080
The first component of data science is statistics,&nbsp;
which shouldn’t be surprising because we’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:41.440
talking about data science. The trick here is&nbsp;
that a lot of things that go into statistics and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:46.160
mathematics can be really counterintuitive, and&nbsp;
if you don’t have the specific formal training,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:51.440
you can make some really big mistakes.
The second element of data science is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:55.520
domain knowledge, and the idea here is that&nbsp;
a researcher should know about the topic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:59.680
area that they’re working in, so if you’re&nbsp;
working in, say for instance, marketing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:04.080
you need to understand how marketing works, and&nbsp;
that makes it so you can have more insight and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:08.240
better direct your analyses and your procedures&nbsp;
to match the questions that you might have.&nbsp;

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:18.080
For instance, there’s a wonderful blog post by&nbsp;
Svetlana Sicular of Gartner Inc, where she writes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:22.800
“Organizations already have people who know their&nbsp;
own data better than mystical data scientists.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:29.280
This is a key. The internal people already&nbsp;
gained experience and ability to model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:35.680
research, and analyze. Learning Hadoop, a common&nbsp;
software or framework for dealing with big data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:39.040
is easier than learning a company’s business.” And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:42.720
that really underscores the importance&nbsp;
of domain knowledge in data science.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:48.960
The third element of Drew Conway’s&nbsp;
data science diagram is coding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:55.840
and this refers to computer programming ability.&nbsp;
Now, it doesn’t need to be complicated. You don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:02.160
have to have a PhD in computer science. A little&nbsp;
bit of Python programming can go a very long way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.760
It’s because this allows for the creative&nbsp;
exploration and manipulation of data sets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:11.440
and especially when you consider the&nbsp;
variety of data that’s part of big data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:17.200
The ability to combine data that comes in&nbsp;
different formats can be a really important thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:21.600
and that often requires some coding&nbsp;
ability. It also helps to develop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:25.840
algorithmic thinking, or thinking in linear&nbsp;
steps-by-steps to get through a problem.&nbsp;

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:32.080
Next, we’ll talk about combinations&nbsp;
of two of these elements at a time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:35.280
The first one is statistics and&nbsp;
domain knowledge without coding.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:40.960
This is what Conway calls traditional research,&nbsp;
and this is where a researcher works within their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:45.840
field of expertise, uses common tools&nbsp;
for working with familiar data formats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:51.200
It’s extremely productive, and nearly all&nbsp;
existing research has been conducted this way.&nbsp;

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:56.800
The second combination is statistics and&nbsp;
coding without substantive expertise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:03.360
This is what Conway refers to as machine learning,&nbsp;
and now, that’s not to be confused with data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:09.760
mining. Machine learning is where an algorithm or&nbsp;
a program updates itself and evolves to perform a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:15.760
specific analytical task. The most familiar&nbsp;
example of this is spam filters in email,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:21.200
in which the user or a whole large group of&nbsp;
users identify messages as spam or not spam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:24.960
and the formula that the program uses&nbsp;
to determine whether something is spam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:29.200
updates with each new piece of information to&nbsp;
have increased accuracy the more you use it.&nbsp;

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:34.640
The third combination here is domain&nbsp;
knowledge and coding without statistics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:40.000
Now, Conway labels this a danger zone,&nbsp;
with the idea being that you have enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:44.720
knowledge to be dangerous. While there are&nbsp;
problems with this, I’ll mention two things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:50.640
First, Conway himself mentions that it seems&nbsp;
very unlikely that a person could develop both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:55.600
programming expertise and substantive knowledge&nbsp;
without also learning some math and statistics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:01.440
so he says it would be a sparsely populated&nbsp;
category, and I believe that’s true. On the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:06.320
hand, there’s some really important data science&nbsp;
contributions that come out of this combination,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:11.760
including, for instance, what are called word&nbsp;
counts, which we’ll talk about later. It’s simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:18.400
stuff. These are procedures that do not require&nbsp;
sophisticated statistics. You’re just counting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:22.960
how often things occur, and you can get important&nbsp;
insights out of that, so I wouldn’t write this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:28.560
one off completely. I would say, though, that&nbsp;
like Conway, it’s not likely that a person could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:33.360
develop expertise in both coding and their domain&nbsp;
without getting the math and statistics as well.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:38.800
And finally, of course, there’s all&nbsp;
three of these things, statistics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:50.160
domain knowledge, and coding, at once, and that&nbsp;
is the most common definition of data science.

